
WP4 sites followed a root exclusion protocol based on trenching,
designed to isolate heterotrophic respiration from total soil
respiration. This approach is particularly valuable for soil carbon
modeling and ecosystem greenhouse gas (GHG) flux quantification. The
trenched area measured 2 m², and soil temperature and moisture were
monitored both inside and outside the trenches using TMS loggers (v4).
Soil CO₂ fluxes were measured biweekly in both control and trenched
plots, except during periods of low microbial activity (e.g., droughts
or winter in northern regions), when measurements were conducted less
frequently.
WP5 sites, in contrast, were established prior to the start of the
project and did not follow the standardized trenching protocol. Instead,
they implemented disturbance treatments representative of common
regional events, such as wildfires and logging, with undisturbed plots
serving as controls. At these sites, soil properties were monitored
throughout the experiment, alongside GHG fluxes and monthly litter
inputs. Soil moisture and temperature were measured using TMS loggers
(v4), as in WP4. However, only total soil CO₂ efflux was measured at WP5
sites. Heterotrophic respiration was thus estimated using the empirical
equation proposed by Bond-Lamberty et al. (2004).

Bond-Lamberty, Ben, ChuankuanWang, and Stith Gower (Oct. 2004). “A
global relationship between the heterotrophic and autotrophic
components of soil respiration?” In: Global Change Biology 10, pp.
1756–1766. DOI: 10.1111/j.1365-2486.2004.00816.x.
